Not neccesarily. Will the police react kindly to the knowledge
that you could have at least shot him in the leg, but let him
go with your stuff?

What's your address? I could use a new VCR. But don't bother,
please, if it's only a two-headed one like the one I already
have.

Seriously, folks:

"Peaceful" thieves that have conducted themselves nonviolently
while performing an inside job on the family business have cost
me and mine a _minimum_ of $ 300,000.00 during the past two
years, as well as a great deal of grief. I won't hesitate to
"peacefully" shoot one in the kneecap.

(The items stolen were worth $ 60,000.00; the damage to our reputation
was on the order of $ 250,000.00. The thief got a forbearance on a $
9,000.00 loan, and a slap on the wrist when a miracle happened and we
found both the pipe and whodunit. The assorted fences, accomplices,
and buyer got the rest of the value, and are currently getting a lawsuit
under the RICO statutes. I don't really agree with them, but when the
state is lax in prosecution, you have to do what you can...)

I intend to write something longer * about this, and why uneven application
of the law isn't a good thing in society (i.e. a class of people with
"pull" who can get prosecutions, and can keep prosecutions from happening
to them, and all of the peons, who have to work in order to get the
people they _know_ stole from them as much as probation): after getting
probation/parole/whatever for the current offense, with the help of
political pull from one of the fences, the perp went ahead and stole
a large bunch of stuff from the Haik family business.

As in Ted Haik, state rep for Iberia Parish, and Dickey Haik,
Federal Judge in district (whatever).

Which is really unfair, I guess, because the Feds refusal to prosecute **
in the first place was allegedly (ah, disclaimers!) upsetting to Judge
Haik long before his brother became a victim himself.


