From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: EXTROPIAN CORRECTNESS

John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us> writes:

>  I'm a little worried. I hope the recent criticism of Phillip
>Zimmermann is not the start of a McCarthy style witch-hint to
>ferret out those who engage in un-Extropian activities. 
>Mr.Zimmermann, while not ideologically pure and whatever his
>personal beliefs has nevertheless done more to advance the ideas
>we value than most of us on this list. Those who protest 
>Mr.Zimmermann's actions should demand that he return all the money
>you paid him for PGP. Perhaps I'm overreacting but I never want
>to read a post that starts "I am holding in my hand a paper with
>the names of 14 members of the Extropy Institute who are card
>carrying members of pro-state organizations".

Agreed.  I thought extropianism -- to the extent that it can be viewed
as an ideology -- presupposed tolerance for people doing their own
thing.  I'd hate to see this list turn into a witch-hunt.

But then, I've got a vested interest, because I'd be one of the people
being hunted.  Y'see, I'm not a libertarian (at least not in the same
sense as most of the people on this list).  Nor do I have much faith
in cryonics.  (Please note the qualifier, "much", before flaming ;-)
Indeed, I'm a dissident with respect to a fair number of the opinions
expressed in this group.  I could 'come out' and reveal other
running-dog counter-revolutionary enemy-of-the-people tendencies, but
I don't want to raise my profile in the BlackNet dossiers; so for now
I'll just say this:

As I see it, the important thing this list is doing is providing a
forum for the open-minded exploration of all possible options for the
future, a place where it's safe to "think the unthinkable" in public.
If we give in to the intellectual bankruptcy of conformity, then the
enterprise will develop a vested interest in not exploring some
possibilities ...  those that are 'unsound' in the context of the
governing ideology.  And because the future is hard to second-guess,
we'll probably miss the boat.


-- Charlie
