In <9310111814.AA19978@spooner>, cappello@cs.ucsb.edu (Peter Cappello)  wrote:

> All along, I have been exploring the boundaries of good taste.  Let's  
> further sharpen the scenario.  There are 2 burglars, one holding your  
> VCR, the other holding your lap-top.  You demand that they sit still  
> until the police arrive.  The one with the VCR clearly states "I am  
> unarmed.  I do not believe that you will shoot me, unarmed as I am,  
> in the back.  I am leaving peacefully with your VCR." 

I say "Up against the wall motherfucker, so that I can
frisk you", using the tone of voice of an enraged home
owner.  If he does not comply I shoot him as in the
previous scenario.  Anybody who claims such vast confidence
in my gentleness and humanity towards burglars is lying his
head off.

If after frisking his corpse, it turns out he really was
unarmed, I say: "Oh dear, it seems that I erred.  Oh well,
not such a terrible loss."

If the one sitting obediently with his hands in the air
moves during all this I shoot him too.

> Is shooting the unarmed burglar in the back _clearly_ what is best  
> for you and your loved ones?

Yes.  But the morally correct course is to tell him to
freeze, then shoot if he moves to turn around.  I think
that I would do the right thing, but I am not entirely sure
that I would do it.  I might well open fire without warning
if I was sure it was a burglar, especially if the range was
short and I was confident of a head shot.

Your next question no doubt is:  Suppose after I frisk him
the burglar then says "Well now you know that I am unarmed, I am
sure you wont shoot me.", and then walks away.

In that case, as in the previous case, I would use
necessary and sufficient force to persuade him to stick
around.  If the burglar was small I would beat him up.  If
he was large, I would kneecap him with the gun and then
beat him up.

